WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.570
I don’t think this video needs an introduction,
so first at the very bottom, this should come

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:08.530
as absolutely no surprise, we have the Battle
of Winterfell.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.799
It’s hilarious that the epic climactic battle
of the entire series both has terrible and

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:18.380
visually disgusting action and a story with
abhorrent usage of plot armor in every single

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.300
nanosecond of screen time.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.109
I could really go off and rail into the story
in depth about why it’s so bad, but I’ll

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:29.590
primarily touch on the action since I’ve
covered most story issues a lot in past videos.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.150
Now the first main thing that’s been brought
up a lot is the visibility of the action.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:38.651
I understand the creators wanted to creatively
show the long night as being very dark, but

00:00:38.651 --> 00:00:40.629
you have to keep the audience in mind.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:44.930
There are certain mechanics within storytelling
like unrealistic lighting that viewers naturally

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.430
accept, as long as it aids the viewing experience.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.180
On a technicallity if you were to shoot in
these conditions, then yes, it would be pitch

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.180
black.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.770
But that’s not how movies and shows operate.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.200
Non-diegetic lighting, which is lighting that
doesn’t have a physical source within the

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.100
world, has become very commonplace and thus
viewers have become used to it.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.070
So in dark environments when you don’t supply
enough non-diegetic lighting, it’s even

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.700
worse because it shatters the norm of lighting
dark settings.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.860
Not to mention there being a lot of fog and
smoke that makes things even harder to see.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.630
So that’s the visibility side of things
which still feels a bit rough even with the

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.290
extra lighting later on.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.670
Then we have the action side of things.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.350
To me, what makes action so entertaining is
complex and great choreography that shows

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.070
off the immense skill of the character at
hand.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.700
In the Battle of Winterfell, there’s only
like two scenes in the entire episode that

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.960
have long-form choreography.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.880
Almost everything is just quick-cut action
where we only linger on a character for a

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.700
few seconds as they cut through a couple wights.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.170
In small doses, this is fine so that you can
instill a sense of chaos, but when most of

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.250
the action is like this, it becomes very tiring.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.240
In large-scale battle sequences, you need
to follow a given character for a decent amount

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.659
of time so that the action is more comprehensible.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:03.200
We can view the action from one continuing
perspective instead of quickly trying to cover

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.969
every single base.

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:09.920
Lots of large-scale action setpieces do this
and an example I’ll use is Helms Deep since

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.040
the creators already compared this battle
to that one, which is pretty sad.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.370
You follow each given hero for a good chunk
of time as they’re met with certain obstacles

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.090
that they have to overcome.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.220
This gives the action more depth and allows
the audience to become more attached to the

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.920
moment as they adore the brilliant choreography.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.340
However, in The Battle of Winterfell, the
only two characters that got this were Arya

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.310
on the wall and Jon Snow’s oner trying to
get to Bran.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:38.720
There are other small moments, but the battle
feels more like they put an emphasis on quantity

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.350
of moments over quality moments.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.590
I think a perfect opportunity to have more
choreography would have been with the white

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.240
walkers entering Winterfell.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.860
Because they have to go through the courtyard
which is where most of our characters are

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:51.860
located.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.290
So having a fight between some of them at
least would have been a major help to the

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.280
overall action quality.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.739
Instead of them being pinned against a wall
perpetually surrounded by Wights where they

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:02.790
should have died a dozen times.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.629
So even the presentation of the action destroys
any suspension of disbelief because they are

00:03:07.629 --> 00:03:10.209
always in scenarios where they should have
died.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:15.040
So overall, those major action issues with
other story issues like the brainless Dothraki

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.120
charge, the terrible defensive layout putting
the trebuchets in the middle of your army

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.410
with no additional trenches, and the death
of the Night King makes this my least favorite

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.530
battle in Game of Thrones.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.080
It’s really depressing.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.310
I’d love to do a scene-by-scene breakdown
and go into all of the numerous issues that

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.890
I didn’t have time to dive into, but we
need to get going.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.099
Second from the bottom, we have the fall of
Kingslanding.

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:41.290
Now the special effects and the spectacle
within this battle are honestly pretty great.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.580
It’s really just the story that absolutely
craters what chances of enjoyment I would

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.360
have had within this battle sequence.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.210
Because if the story wasn’t completely braindead
then I’d probably really enjoy this battle.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:57.519
However, that isn’t the case and the massive
elephant in the room is Dany’s turn.

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:01.670
I’ve already explained in many videos about
why her arc was butchered here, the most recent

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.950
one being the Top 10 most ruined characters,
so I don’t want to spend a lot of time going

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.220
over that again.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.470
Outside of Dany’s arc there’s your usual
flair of contrived writing and plot armor.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.810
The contrived aspect that really drives me
up a wall whenever I rewatch this episode

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:21.040
is the fact that the scorpions turn into incompetent
weapons being manned by people who are mentally

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.040
AFK.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.760
And I’m not talking about Dany destroying
the fleet which is a whole other issue since

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.009
there’s like a hundred ships and she could
barely handle less than a dozen the episode

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:35.230
before, but the entire city’s defenses not
even moving to shoot at her is unacceptable.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.669
When previously in the story you make it to
where a manned scorpion has the range and

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:43.290
accuracy of a world-class sniper, you then
can’t make them comically ineffective.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.650
It just screams contriveness.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.140
Then there’s the other dumb points like
Arya dying multiple times and Euron teleporting

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.950
to Jaime’s exact position in space and time.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.640
So it’s not just Daenyers snapping which
is what a lot of people assume what’s wrong

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:57.640
with this battle.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:03.639
Instead, it’s a constant stream of dumb
writing beats that sink this battle lower

00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:04.639
and lower.

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:07.500
Then I said the spectacle was pretty great,
but the action within the spectacle is really

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.450
nothing special and is at times pretty bad.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.330
Like the fight between Jaime and Euron is
flat out jarring and even has a moment that

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.490
breaks continuity.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.639
On top of there not really being any other
fight scenes besides The Mountain vs. the

00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:23.009
hound which is really the only saving grace
of this battle sequence.

00:05:23.009 --> 00:05:27.220
So in essence, there may be some gorgeous
spectacle within this battle that can wow

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.810
you, but it feels weightless because of how
much its robbed by the writing.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.330
At number 8 we have the Siege of Mereen.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:38.820
Initially, I wasn’t even thinking about
counting this as a battle because of how fast

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.310
and anticlimactic it was, but it technically
counts.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.810
It’s pretty crazy that this battle is smooshed
into the Battle of the Bastards episode.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.660
Like you can really tell how fast David and
Dan are rushing to try and resolve everything

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.120
in Mereen so that Dany can head to Westeros.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.750
If anything, it feels like this battle should
have had at least half of an episode on its

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:03.310
own because it’s literally the climax of
a plotline that lasted almost three seasons.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.630
Let that sink in.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:08.849
David and Dan resolved this major climactic
point in less than 10 minutes.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:12.669
This is probably the biggest example of rushing
along the story.

00:06:12.669 --> 00:06:17.569
Now from a technical standpoint, Dany can
easily smash the masters because she has dragons

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:19.599
and two separate large armies.

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:24.160
So you may be thinking, well Supercuts Delight
the battle is so fast because Daenerys made

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.280
slight work of taking out the masters, but
why the hell would the masters do this then.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.980
They’re yielding magnificent results by
just using the Sons of the Harpy to cause

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:35.169
mass unrest within Mereen.

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:38.280
Like Tyrion genuinely didn’t know how to
stop what was happening.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.690
As you can see in the beginning of season
6, Mereen is basically a ghost town entrenched

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.380
in misery and fear.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:48.640
The second the Masters withdraw the Sons of
the Harpy, Mereen instantly explodes with

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.250
life and social prosperity.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:55.910
So the question stands in that why would the
Masters not continue doing this and watch

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.379
the city slowly die and wither away.

00:06:58.379 --> 00:07:02.470
Especially since they know that Daenerys will
be leaving to Westeros soon, which is also

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.830
confusing as to why they destroyed her fleet.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:08.250
And instead, they decide to use the Sons of
the Harpy, who are just regular people with

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:12.530
no fighting experience, as a full-force army
to take over the city.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:17.500
So David and Dan are essentially jerry-rigging
the story into a nonsensical direction so

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.530
that it can quickly end and Dany can move
on without any burdens.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:25.849
Because unsurprisingly, the Sons of the Harpy
are quickly snuffed out by Dany’s army and

00:07:25.849 --> 00:07:29.220
she uses her dragons to instantly make them
surrender.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:33.629
Watching her torch a ship with her three dragons
is a satisfying moment with some payoff, but

00:07:33.629 --> 00:07:37.340
that’s really the only positive aspect of
this entire sequence.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:40.919
The only reason why I wouldn’t put this
lower in the battle ranking is because the

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:46.199
writing doesn’t infuriate me as much as
The Battle of Winterfell and the Fall of Kingslanding.

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:50.129
It's just here and blending into the corner
as you forget about it since The Battle of

00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:54.419
the Bastards is much more memorable and overshadows
it completely.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:57.780
For number 7 we have the Iron Fleet surprise
attack.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.190
This is again another small battle that I
would count because of it’s large scale

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.000
and it’s an actual scene.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.759
Unlike the assault on Casterly Rock which
is essentially just a montage.

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:09.949
The main reason that I’m putting this battle
low on this list is because it’s really

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:13.100
short and there aren’t a ton of impressive
aspects about it.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:16.520
It’s mostly generic quick cut action which
gets the job done.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.250
Then there’s one satisfying choreographed
scene where Euron kills the sand snakes.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:24.229
This feels kind of fan servicey because of
the fan hatred towards them, but it was still

00:08:24.229 --> 00:08:25.479
good nonetheless.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:29.360
In particular, I liked the variety in weapon
combat in terms of Euron countering the spear

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:30.450
and the whip.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.729
Then there’s a few minor writing flaws like
Euron’s fleet being able to sneak up on

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:38.200
them so easily, and them kind of just leaving
Theon Greyjoy behind to live.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:43.220
How generous of Euron to allow Theon the opportunity
to come back and save his sister.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.620
Since this scene is 5 minutes there’s not
a ton to talk about so yeah.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.290
Onto number 6 I’m kind of cheating a little
bit, but it’s the Battle of the Stepstones.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:55.110
The video does say Game of Thrones but I’ll
rope in House of the Dragon just to see where

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.720
this battle ranks in comparison to the others.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.620
This may seem relatively low, but keep in
mind it’s kind of hard to compete against

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.720
other battles that are much bigger and longer.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.500
Because that’s the main downside to this
battle in that it only lasts for 3 minutes

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.339
excluding the Daemon rush.

00:09:10.339 --> 00:09:14.190
Which going into that, I still see a lot of
people knock it for having plot armor and

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:16.459
I still stand by the fact that it’s believable.

00:09:16.459 --> 00:09:20.720
The archers are very far away trying to hit
a target in full sprint, moving erratically,

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.529
and taking cover occasionally.

00:09:22.529 --> 00:09:26.279
So the fact that he is hit with 3 arrows I
might add since people act like he doesn’t

00:09:26.279 --> 00:09:27.670
get hit once, is fine.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:31.790
With that out of the way I really like the
concept of Daemon doing an all out rush for

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:32.790
his life.

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:36.300
It’s infused with high adrenaline that is
severely aided by the fact that there’s

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.279
a lot of buildup to this moment.

00:09:38.279 --> 00:09:43.160
Ramin Djwadi also helps the scene with his
haunting score that then explodes simultaneously

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.160
with the action.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.540
I really love the progression of this scene
as he just barrels through people and eviscerates

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:49.540
them.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:53.330
Makes him come across as such a badass and
thus makes him an even cooler character.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:56.470
This paired with a short battle right after
I think at least puts this in the middle of

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:57.600
my ranking.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:02.640
There are some shots with some wonky VFX like
the blood effects oddly looking bad, but like

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.500
usual, this battle hosts a lot of chaotic
energy.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:09.360
It was cool though to see Lord Corlys rip
through people and gave the action another

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.790
fun perspective to follow in the mayhem.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.620
At number 5 it’s the Spoils of War or The
Loot Train Attack.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:19.720
Despite this battle taking place in a rather
bad season of the show, it still manages to

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:20.930
be pretty good.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:24.810
It does have its flaws obviously, but it has
some high points for action.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.740
The initial breach of Dany lighting up over
a dozen people with her Dragon is one of the

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.750
most iconic moments in any battle sequence.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:35.540
It just hits you with an orgasmic rush of
satisfaction because we’ve been waiting

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.320
for this kind of moment for 6 seasons.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.850
Along with the execution being brutal and
practical.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:44.870
I’m really happy that most of the fire stunts
weren’t done digitally and it makes the

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:46.920
action so much more authentic.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.040
Your jaw hits the floor because you’ve never
seen anything on this scale before.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.600
Then for the Dothraki, the action has an interesting
dynamic because you see two completely different

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.279
styles of fighting clash together.

00:10:57.279 --> 00:11:01.180
And a style that severely counters the other
as Robert Baratheon said.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:05.980
We see some cool variety like Dothraki shooting
arrows from horseback, dual-wielding arakhs,

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:10.390
and their superior dexterity and speed as
they take down multiple Lannister soldiers

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:11.470
before dying.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.010
I’ve also kind of skipped over it, but the
beginning of this battle is an outstanding

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.000
example of perfect buildup.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.339
It first interrupts a normal dialogue scene
with a distant rumbling of horse hooves, to

00:11:21.339 --> 00:11:25.410
the frantic realization that they are getting
rolled up on and they need to quickly form

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:30.320
a tangible defense, to the reveal of the massive
army coming over the horizon in which the

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.760
heroes realize they’re screwed, and then
the even bigger reveal of a dragon heading

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:38.261
right for them and it’s about to turn into
a bloodbath.

00:11:38.261 --> 00:11:42.050
It’s literally a perfect escalation that
gets you really hyped and reminds me of the

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:45.250
opening battle in The Mummy which I adored
growing up.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:49.410
Also in the build-up, I love the quick cuts
of Lannister soldiers shitting their pants.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:53.150
It really allows us to sympathize with them
and how much fear they must be feeling.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:57.130
Then from a technical standpoint, the battle
is really impressive to me because you have

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.560
to simultaneously juggle the ground assault
and the air assault.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.250
I think the creators found a good balance
in terms of presenting the battle because

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.480
it could have been very incoherent.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.551
It flows really well and even the quick moments
of action have more time to breathe and aren’t

00:12:10.551 --> 00:12:14.380
just quickly thrown in to artificially make
the edit more chaotic.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:18.290
Then one last thing about the action before
going into the writing side of things, I think

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:22.220
the oner with Bronn in the middle of the battlefield
was a great creative decision.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:27.000
It geniusely shows us this uninterrupted hellish
experience of dragon warfare and allows the

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:31.540
audience to watch in horror as to how brutal
and crazy this must be to go through.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:35.360
Like I’d even be scared to go through this
if I was fighting for Daenyers.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:39.070
So for the writing, there are a few nitpicks
like why didn’t Dany do a strafe run down

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:43.540
the defensive line of Lannisters, and why
she destroyed the food instead of capturing

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.680
it, but those are pretty minor to me and the
ending is much worse in my opinion.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.380
David and Dan simultaneously wanted to keep
Jaime Lannister alive, but they also wanted

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:53.920
him to be in the battle.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:58.230
So getting him out alive just fundamentally
wouldn’t make sense and Bronn tackling him

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.040
into the water and miraculously swimming him
an entire lake distance away is ridiculous.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.870
Not to mention that Jaime is wearing full-plated
armor.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:09.570
It sucks that David and Dan couldn’t come
up with a better way to get around it.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:13.940
Just thinking about it now, a significantly
better change would be to have Jaime become

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:17.850
traumatized by watching his men getting torched
and have him simply run away.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:23.010
Then he can deal with the burden of abandoning
his post and the betrayal of his men, which

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.750
eats him alive internally.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:28.880
This could also be a contributing factor for
him going North to fight for the living because

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:32.040
he wants to make amends for his cowardice
in the past.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:36.070
Not him teleporting out of the battle so he
can artificially remain in the story.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:40.320
So it’s pretty unfortunate that the ending
to this battle sucks because if that wasn’t

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.360
the case then I’d put it higher.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.570
Onto number 4 we have The Battle of the Bastards.

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.480
Now this may seem like heresy putting what’s
regarded as the best battle in the show at

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.620
number 4, but hear me out.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:56.190
If it was just from the perspective of action
then this battle would definitely be higher.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:14:00.800
However, you have to also consider the story
side of things because that aspect drags this

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:03.720
battle down like Jaime sinking to the bottom
of the lake.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.660
There’s sadly so many aspects wrong with
this like one of the main ones being Sansa

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:11.720
not telling Jon about the vale which would
have led to an easy winning strategy of having

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.550
much higher numbers.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:17.110
Then another thing about this that people
don’t consider is that if Sansa confirmed

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:21.509
earlier that she had their support, then most
of the other northern houses who were hesitant

00:14:21.509 --> 00:14:23.690
to join, most likely would have.

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:26.660
Which would increase their chances of winning
even more.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:31.420
Instead, David and Dan create a contrived
sense of an underdog conflict for more drama.

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:36.440
The Vale magically arrive at the exact moment
of Jon’s defeat and this may be a nitpick,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:40.230
but how would this large army even be a surprise
to the Boltons.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:43.920
Is there no such thing anymore as scouts reporting
on enemy movements?

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.970
Like what happened to the times in the earlier
seasons where Tywin and Rob would get constant

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.020
reports on one another's armies.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:55.300
Hell, even Brienne escorting Jaime across
the country got spotted by a random peasant.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:56.300
But now?

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:00.380
Armies are teleporting around with literally
no strategic or geographical depth.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:04.870
Then there’s your obvious instances of plot
armor of Jon becoming a god and surviving

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.840
dozens of barrages and cavalry charges.

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:11.570
With this critique, I’m not saying that
I want him to die, but instead I want him

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:16.529
to sustain some kind of injury because if
he can survive this, then you ruin the realism

00:15:16.529 --> 00:15:19.880
and sense of danger that were set in the earlier
seasons.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.880
There are some other minor issues like Wun
Wun basically going into the war naked and

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:28.720
with no weapons, the mostly idiotic military
strategy, there not really being any characters

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:32.281
on the villain side fighting that you care
about, within 2 minutes of the battle there

00:15:32.281 --> 00:15:36.670
are massive piles of bodies, along with it
being confusing as to why it’s so condensed

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:40.730
when everyone is spread out, and people who
don’t understand the concept of not running

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:42.200
in a straight line.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:46.819
But heyyyy, now that we got past that massive
tsunami of dogshit, we can finally appreciate

00:15:46.819 --> 00:15:49.250
the gorgeous action on display.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:52.140
Because really everything is firing on all
cylinders.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:56.900
The cinematography is breathtaking and establishes
some of the most iconic shots in the entire

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:02.399
series of Jon facing down an entire cavalry
charge, the juxtaposing singles of Jon and

00:16:02.399 --> 00:16:07.850
Ramsey, the POV of an arrow barrage, the insane
oner with clever ways of hiding cuts in the

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:12.810
chaos, and a brilliant top down shot of jon
remerging as if he’s being reborn.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:16.069
Drawing genius parallels to Dany’s ending
in season 3.

00:16:16.069 --> 00:16:20.440
Fun fact, this moment wasn’t actually planned
and was something that Miguel Sapochnik came

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:22.269
up with the night before shooting.

00:16:22.269 --> 00:16:26.910
I also love the bleak, dour, and cold color
correction for the overall episode.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:31.290
Looking specifically at the action, I think
it’s a good combination of frantic cuts

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:34.790
with quick action supplemented with Jon’s
legendary oner.

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:38.400
It’d be nice if this section of the battle
was a bit longer and we could see how Jon

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.139
was surviving this chaos more, but it’s
still great nonetheless.

00:16:42.139 --> 00:16:46.100
The overall production value and what they
were able to achieve with an 11 million dollar

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:48.670
budget is insanely impressive.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:53.630
This has become a golden standard of excellent
VFX integrated with practical elements.

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:57.940
If I were to pause this frame you probably
wouldn’t be able to tell what’s real and

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:58.940
fake.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:02.920
But yeah, the entire battle is amazing from
an action perspective and it’s really sad

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:05.850
that it gets held back by the shoddy writing.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.370
At number 3 we have Hardhome.

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:12.610
Unlike battles before this, I honestly can’t
think of any big flaws that deteriorate my

00:17:12.610 --> 00:17:14.110
enjoyment of this.

00:17:14.110 --> 00:17:18.130
It completely comes out of nowhere and the
pure excitement you feel from being this surprised

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:21.390
by a sudden battle was truly a special experience.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:25.650
It absolutely floors you as all of the characters
are left extremely vulnerable.

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:30.679
Miguel Sapachonik perfectly captures the panic
and hysteria of people being terrified and

00:17:30.679 --> 00:17:35.400
I think hiding the undead behind the wall
for a period of time adds to the horror buildup.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:39.440
That and it acts as a good way to naturally
slow down the enemy so that they don’t immediately

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:41.140
destroy our characters.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:45.289
Now the action is predominantly quick-cut
and fast-paced, but I think that’s much

00:17:45.289 --> 00:17:49.010
more justified because this isn’t a battle,
it’s a massacre.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:53.020
We do get some occasional choreographed moments
and in particular, I love the tracking shot

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.549
of Jon running through the battle and doing
a 360 chop on a wight.

00:17:56.549 --> 00:18:01.670
We also get to see Wun Wun absolutely obliterate
his foes for the first time which is amazing

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:02.670
too.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.270
Then besides most of this chaos we get a duel
between Jon and a white walker.

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:11.000
The introduction of the white walker in particular
was so badass and perfectly framed.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.380
Along with it having thematic significance
of fire and ice.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:18.669
But besides this opening shot, the white walker
destroying this seemingly powerful character

00:18:18.669 --> 00:18:20.940
makes you think Jon is screwed for the fight.

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:25.559
And he pretty much is and kind of wins due
to a lucky instance in that he caught the

00:18:25.559 --> 00:18:29.090
white walker off guard since he was expecting
his sword to shatter.

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.789
This paired with a handful of magnificent
moments like the undead barreling off the

00:18:32.789 --> 00:18:37.530
cliff, Karsi getting rekt by wight children,
our heroes running together in unity with

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:42.179
that great trio shot, and the wall falling
down make this a consistently riveting action

00:18:42.179 --> 00:18:43.390
setpiece.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:48.559
Not to mention the best ending to any battle
sequence on this list being the iconic resurrection

00:18:48.559 --> 00:18:49.559
scene.

00:18:49.559 --> 00:18:53.090
So yeah, I came really close to putting this
battle at number 2, but I feel like the next

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.610
two have more to offer than Hardhome.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:58.370
For Number 2 we have the Blackwater.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:02.210
Before going into the action, I want to point
out how expertly crafted the buildup to this

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:03.660
battle was.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.530
Because through the entire season, there’s
this constant dread in the air that Kinglsanding

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:11.870
will essentially be helpless if Stannis decides
to attack them outright.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:16.330
Even leading right up to the battle there’s
this nauseating feeling of helplessness accentuated

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:17.330
by different characters.

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:22.750
Then it’s all relieved by an immensely clever
action made by Tyrion in blowing up Stannis’

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:23.750
fleet.

00:19:23.750 --> 00:19:27.720
This adds to that euphoric rush of relieving
that defeated mentality, along with this moment

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:31.880
just being jaw-dropping from how impressive
the visuals and VFX are.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:36.039
It kind of reminds me of the initial strafe
that Daenerys does, but this moment has way

00:19:36.039 --> 00:19:38.799
more build-up and story significance.

00:19:38.799 --> 00:19:41.919
This moment leads to a more triumphant feeling
and throughout the battle, there’s this

00:19:41.919 --> 00:19:45.580
ebb and flow of going from a winning and losing
mentality.

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:49.010
Because even though this explosion evened
the odds a bit, there’s still a daunting

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:53.000
task in front of them as they still have another
half of Stannis’ army to repel.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.530
This makes the battle story-wise feel a lot
more dynamic and unpredictable as to who you

00:19:57.530 --> 00:19:58.590
think will win.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.820
Even though Tywin is technically on the way.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:04.840
Then for the action, there’s technically
not a lot of it and this is probably a bit

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:08.180
of a budgetary issue, but what they captured
was still good.

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:13.340
They infused it with enough quantity of carnage
to capture the essence of a crazy battle interspersed

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:15.640
with the Hound absolutely obliterating people.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.290
I honestly could just watch that for hours.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:22.720
The highlight of this battle to me however,
besides the explosion, is the great character

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:23.720
work.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:27.309
Every major character here essentially has
an arc of sorts between the Hound finally

00:20:27.309 --> 00:20:32.049
defecting and leaving, Joffrey wimping out
and showing how much of a coward he is, and

00:20:32.049 --> 00:20:36.210
Tyrion rising to the occasion and becoming
the savior of Kingslanding.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:39.320
Essentially dismantling all the hateful notions
people made towards him.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:43.549
That kind of emotional rush for the character
is so moving that you can’t help but feel

00:20:43.549 --> 00:20:45.230
incredibly excited.

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:49.059
There is some more character work with Sansa
and Cersei, but I honestly found that their

00:20:49.059 --> 00:20:51.380
scenes hurt the pacing of the battle.

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:55.280
Their scenes are good, but an unfortunate
side effect is the antsy feeling of wanting

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:56.860
to get back to the action.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:21:00.510
It kind of feels like these interjected scenes
were more of a way to pad out the length of

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:03.679
the battle because of the relatively small
budget.

00:21:03.679 --> 00:21:07.610
Then the scale was still impressive with so
many wonderful wideshots of rushing the beach

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:08.900
and scaling the walls.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:13.770
And heyyy, a character actually sustains some
kind of major injury and they didn’t even

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.360
make a complete blunder like Jon Snow.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:21.029
It still pisses me off that he faces no consequences
for his mistake of falling into Ramsey’s

00:21:21.029 --> 00:21:25.470
trap, but yeah, the Blackwater is an outstanding
battle and is the first time in which we get

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:29.120
to witness an action scene on this magnificent
scale.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:33.040
Finally, at number 1 we have The Watchers
on the Wall.

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.750
This really shouldn’t be a surprise because
this was also my favorite episode.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.520
The Watchers on the Wall is perfect from every
single aspect.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:48.700
Whether it be scale, characters, action, choreography,
story beats, acting, dialogue, the great setting,

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:49.700
and cinematography.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.320
It’s all flawless.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:55.360
From a story perspective, the plot and characters
are incredibly investing because this has

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.799
been building up for multiple seasons.

00:21:57.799 --> 00:22:01.570
There are so much emotional stakes charged
for each character whether that be for Ser

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:06.630
Alliser desperately defending his home and
not wanting to be the first to fall to Wildlings,

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:11.480
the conflicted nature of Rose in that she’s
angry at Jon but simultaneously still loves

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:15.610
him, or Jon being torn between killing these
wildlings that aren’t the enemy.

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:19.390
It just feels like a tragedy for him in that
he has to defend his brothers from people

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:21.159
who should be their allies.

00:22:21.159 --> 00:22:25.440
So the story and characters in this battle
make every scene thoroughly engaging because

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:27.490
both sides are considered as the good guys.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:32.610
This creates a lot more emotional and story
depth because the conflict isn’t black and

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:33.610
white.

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:36.940
It’s very complex and I like it more than
future battles that are essentially just good

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:38.210
versus evil.

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:40.850
Then of course the action is hard-peaking
here.

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:46.130
Like the iconic oner of the entire courtyard
and longer take duels between different characters.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:49.850
The Watchers on the Wall is also really the
first and only battle when you think about

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:51.830
it that has character duels.

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:55.420
Where we have Tormund vs Ser Alliser and Jon
vs Styr.

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:59.330
The only other battles that has a character
duel is in The Bells between Jaimie and Euron

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:03.620
which is just yeah, and then Battle of the
Bastards where Tormund kills Smalljon who

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:06.169
really didn’t have any characterization.

00:23:06.169 --> 00:23:08.950
And Jon killing Ramsay which was more of a
butchering.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:13.370
I think character duels creates a great dynamic
in which you have stakes on both sides of

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:14.370
the action.

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:18.640
Like you kind of wanting Tormund and Alliser
to survive and for Jon to kill Styr.

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:22.039
Instead of the generic mowing down of random
unnamed enemies.

00:23:22.039 --> 00:23:25.140
It actually creates an investment for this
action scene.

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:29.270
Then there are so many iconic moments like
a giant impaling someone with his bow and

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:34.179
arrow, dropping the scythe, a giant riding
a mammoth, and the boys coming together to

00:23:34.179 --> 00:23:36.270
take down a big chongus.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:40.470
It makes the battle consistently exciting
as it offers fresh and fun moments.

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:43.919
You can really tell that the budget is much
higher here than the Blackwater and makes

00:23:43.919 --> 00:23:48.030
me wonder how much better that battle sequence
would have been with a bigger budget.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:51.669
Because this battle just keeps you in a constant
state of amazement because the background

00:23:51.669 --> 00:23:56.990
action is always dialed up to 11 and there
is so much VFX casually being used to great

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:57.990
effect.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:00.830
Then this is something that probably isn’t
brought up a lot, but I think the pacing of

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:02.510
this battle is spectacular.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:06.250
Because most battles start and are essentially
a full sprint to the finish, but the Watchers

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:07.370
on the Wall feels like a marathon.

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:11.770
There are a lot of great character beats planted
throughout this battle like Samwell becoming

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:17.040
more brave and even helping a scared Pip,
Jon and Sir Alliser’s delightful conversation,

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.409
and different characters getting to take charge.

00:24:19.409 --> 00:24:22.710
Which essentially gives some cooldown time
between certain action beats.

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:27.020
That and the war speech from Sir Alliser is
fucking amazing and I just adore his character.

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:31.070
Also, I haven’t even touched on the tragic
ending of Jon losing Rose.

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:35.460
The battle ends with such a dramatic gut punch
and the convention break in which the creators

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.429
use slow motion for the first time was genius.

00:24:38.429 --> 00:24:43.420
It links us into Jon’s headspace as we are
solely focused on his misery amongst the chaos

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:44.660
surrounding him.

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:48.710
I can really talk about this masterpiece of
a battle for like an hour and why it’s so

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.860
amazing, but yeah, for the sake of your time
I’ll end it here.

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:55.020
Thank you for watching and let me know in
the comments what your ranking would be.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:24:58.840
I’m genuinely curious because I know a lot
of people’s favorite battle tends to be

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:01.970
the Battle of the Bastards and I’m curious
what you guys think.

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:04.429
And by the way, this video was voted on by
my patrons, have a good day.

